\paragraph{Running the knee experiment.}
From the repository root in a fresh MATLAB session with Simulink, run the commands below. They copy the saved library, knee model, and driver into a new temporary directory so that the driver can write its instrumented model and simulation results there. The argument \texttt{reader} selects the broadened ramps. The measurements above were reproduced in MATLAB R2025a Update~1.

\begingroup\small\singlespacing
\begin{verbatim}
s = fullfile(pwd,"Code","Matlab","SNS_Simscape","results");
s = fullfile(s,"knee_transient_reflex_20261028");
b = fullfile(s,"original_snapshot");
w = tempname; d = fullfile(w,"demos");
p = fullfile(w,"results","knee");
mkdir(d); mkdir(p);
copyfile(fullfile(b,"SNS_Library.slx"),w);
copyfile(fullfile(b,"KneeReflexDemo.slx"),d);
copyfile(fullfile(s,"run_transient.m"),p);
addpath(p);
run_transient(char("reader"));
m = jsondecode(fileread(fullfile(p,"reader_summary.json")));
ref = jsondecode(fileread(fullfile(s,"replay_summary.json")));
isequaln(m,ref)

The model completed \qty{28}{\second}. During $16\leq t<28$\,s, mean vertical harness force was \qty{570.20625}{
ewton}, or \qty{77.5}{\percent} of the \qty{735.75}{
ewton} weight of the \qty{75}{\kilogram} model. Seven right and six left unloaded, whole-foot-clear intervals gave twelve alternations. Each interval required foot-mesh clearance above \qty{5}{\milli\meter}, ipsilateral normal load below \qty{7.36}{
ewton}, contralateral load above \qty{15}{
ewton}, and duration of at least \qty{80}{\milli\second}. The \qty{2}{\milli\second} guard calculation recorded whole-trial minimum anatomical flexion of \ang{11.20} right and \ang{5.10} left against a \ang{1} limit. Figure~ef{fig:app_cad_supported_traces} shows the signed native angle sampled every \qty{10}{\milli\second}, which can miss a physics-step extremum. Whole-trial world-forward displacement was \qty{-74.7}{\milli\meter} with orientation and lateral guidance active.

The active-set record also determines the bend measure used in the wrapping loss of Equations~\eqref{eq:deltaL} and~\eqref{eq:x3}. For the femoral-cylinder, tibial, and ICR crossing-point contacts, the arc length is $R_f\alpha_f$, the effective radius times the turn angle in radians. Along the femoral condyle the radius varies, so its contribution $s_{\mathrm{condyle}}$ follows the polar curve. The total is
\begin{equation}
b(\theta_{\mathbf{k}}) = \sum_{f\ne\mathrm{condyle}} R_f\alpha_f + s_{\mathrm{condyle}}, \qquad
s_{\mathrm{condyle}} = \int \sqrt{r(\vartheta)^2+\left(\mathrm{d}r/\mathrm{d}\vartheta\right)^2}\,\mathrm{d}\vartheta,
\label{eq:app_bend_measure}
\end{equation}
where each contribution is a length at the specified knee angle and the integral spans the active condyle arc. Each family's turn angle ramps between the route-state events that bound it and reaches a plateau at full engagement. Applying the same release rule to another route requires an ordered set of sagittal contacts, one segment crossing between the two bodies, body transforms and clearance envelopes at every pose, and a sweep in which contacts release monotonically.

\subsection{Verification Gates of the MuJoCo Mirror}

Table~\ref{tab:app_spk_gates} gives the tests from Section~\ref{sec:spiking_methods}. The graded and spiking networks retained the same directed connections under each of three gain settings. Under constant drive the spiking rhythm alternated between the legs, but its period exceeded the specified tolerance. With the model suspended, the spiking network also produced finite joint motion at a different cadence from the graded network. The scripts, logs, and numerical results are archived in the reports folder (\nolinkurl{tools/}, \nolinkurl{logs/}, and the \texttt{json} files).

